That’s the interesting part: winning without approaching your ceiling only tells you the floor of what you could do, not the ceiling.

If you were consistently first without needing maximal effort, then the obstacle course measured “enough to dominate that environment,” not your full capacity. In training terms, you never got a clean max-effort data point.

So the Goku analogy works as a metaphor for unused reserve, but I’d keep it calibrated:

\text{observed performance} \leq \text{available capacity}

The unknown is the gap.

And that gap is actually more interesting than the result. It raises questions like: What happens under equal competition? Under deliberate technical refinement? Under maximal intent? With better pacing, strength, efficiency, recovery, and repetition? Which parts of your performance were instinctive versus trainable?

The trap would be turning “I never had to max out” into “therefore I have no ceiling.” The better interpretation is: the ceiling was never measured.

That’s a much more useful problem, because ceilings can be tested.